/// Group 1 to n by cutting at sample quantiles; tied values share a group.
///
/// The group each value falls in when the column is cut at its sample quantiles: with `n: 4` the
/// quartile groups 1 to 4, with `n: 10` the deciles. The cut points are the quantiles at 1/n to
/// (n − 1)/n under the definition `type:` names (the nine of Hyndman and Fan, as `quantile`
/// offers them; 7 unless given, R's default), and a value equal to a cut point falls in the group
/// below it, so that tied values always share a group, as R's `cut(x, quantile(x, probs),
/// include.lowest = TRUE)` places them. `NA` stays `NA`, and the quantiles are taken over the
/// values present. Under `by:` the cut points are taken within each group. Like Stata's `xtile`.
///
/// x: A variable of Ints or Floats.
/// n: The number of groups, a whole number of at least 1.
/// type: The quantile definition, 1 to 9; 7 unless given.
/// returns: A column of Ints from 1 to `n`, one per row, `NA` where `x` is.
/// details: Closed form: the cut points are the kernel's sample quantiles, the same bits as `quantile` gives, at the probabilities i/n each computed as the one division, and each row's group is one more than the number of cut points below its value. When the column has fewer distinct values than groups, two cut points coincide, the groups between them are empty and the numbering is kept, as Stata's `xtile` keeps it; R's `cut` refuses repeated breaks instead.
/// reference: Hyndman, R. J. and Fan, Y. (1996), Sample quantiles in statistical packages, The American Statistician 50(4), 361–365. Stata, the `xtile` command.
/// see also: quantile, bin, rank, winsorize
/// > let d = frame(x = [5, 1, 9, 3, 7, NA, 2, 8])
/// > d.add(quartile = quantile_group(x, n: 4), third = quantile_group(x, n: 3, type: 1))
/// frame · 8 rows × 3 variables
///
///        x   quartile   third
///   1    5          2       2
///   2    1          1       1
///   3    9          4       3
///   4    3          2       1
///   5    7          3       2
///   6   NA         NA      NA
///   7    2          1       1
///   8    8          4       3
/// > frame(x = [1, 1, 1, 1, 2]).add(q = quantile_group(x, n: 4))
/// frame · 5 rows × 2 variables
///
///       x   q
///   1   1   1
///   2   1   1
///   3   1   1
///   4   1   1
///   5   2   4
fn quantile_group(x: Column<Float>, n: Int?, `type`: Int = 7) -> Column<Int> {
    if is_missing(n) {
        error("`quantile_group` needs `n:`; `quantile_group(x, n: 4)` gives quartile groups 1 to 4 and `n: 10` deciles")
    }
    if n < 1 {
        error("`n:` of `quantile_group` is a count of groups of at least 1")
    }
    if `type` < 1 || `type` > 9 {
        error("`type:` of `quantile_group` must be one of the nine definitions, 1 to 9; `type: 7` is R's default")
    }
    // Each row's group is one more than the number of cut points below its value; NA stays NA.
    let below = case { is_missing(x) => NA, _ => 0 }
    if n == 1 {
        return 1 + below
    }
    // zeff: allow(W0201) the cut points are taken over the values present, as the page says
    let cuts = frame(value = x).quantile(value, p: map(seq(1, n - 1), fn(i) i / n), type: `type`)
    1 + reduce(cuts, fn(acc, c) acc + to(c < x, Int), below)
}
